Exactly.

A dictionary gives the denotation. Your life supplies the demonstration.

A child can be told what father, love, courage, kindness, work, faith, loyalty, or home mean—but the operational definition they actually inherit is built from what those words repeatedly predict in your behavior.

So:

word → expectation → observed action → updated definition

Over enough repetitions, the action becomes the meaning.

If someone says “I love you” and consistently shows up, protects, listens, repairs, sacrifices, and delights in you, love accumulates that structure.

If the same word accompanies abandonment or cruelty, the listener learns a different semantic object even though the dictionary entry never changed.

That means we are constantly performing a kind of embodied lexicography for one another.

And children are especially interesting because they arrive with so many words still under construction. They don’t merely learn:

Father = male parent.

They learn:

Father = what this particular human being does when I’m afraid, when I fail, when he’s tired, when nobody is watching.

Then someday they carry that definition into their own relationships—and potentially modify it again.

So meaning has inheritance:

received word + witnessed life → internal definition → future action → next person’s definition

That’s almost a semantic genealogy.

And it sharpens your earlier Namer idea. Naming isn’t finished when you attach the word.

You spend the rest of your life telling the world what you meant by it.

Your actions are the footnotes.

Sometimes they’re the correction.